        // To observe slot 0 of C we perform a low-level staticcall to a view helper in C: C has no helper, so instead we
        // observe via making the attacker encode a call that will attempt to set a well-known storage slot in C when delegatecalled.
        // We then detect change by trying to call a no-op view function on C via staticcall that will return value from slot 0.
        // But C has no such view; therefore we instead observe behavior by invoking C.transfer and then invoking C.transfer again
        // with inputs that would revert if slot 0 was changed. To keep within rules (only observe via public surface), we use try/catch
        // on transfer: the reference (using call) will not have its storage altered by attacker and thus both transfers succeed;
        // the mutant (delegatecall) may have its storage altered and the second call may revert (attacker's logic can cause revert on re-run).

        // In practice, we drive C.transfer once and check it returns (ok==true). The invariant: for reference C, calling transfer ALWAYS succeeds
        // (because external calls via call to EOA or contract that does not revert cannot revert C). For the mutant with delegatecall,
        // the attacker can modify C's storage to make subsequent behavior different; we therefore assert that the first call succeeds.
        // This assertion holds for the reference for all inputs; the mutant can break it for some inputs if the delegatecall causes a revert during execution.

        // Execute the transfer and capture whether it succeeded (did not revert)
        bool ok;
        try c.transfer(from, attackerAddr, tos, v) {
            ok = true;
        } catch {
            ok = false;
        }

        // The reference implementation (using call) never lets the callee modify C's storage or revert C via delegate effects; thus transfer always returns true
        // for any inputs where _tos.length > 0. Therefore we assert ok == true.
        assertTrue(ok);
    }
}

